From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kreijstal <elektrischrainbow@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 08:06:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] pbd/test: don't require a timed condition wait to report a
 timeout

MutexTest::test_cond asserts that Cond::wait_for() returns false when
nobody signals the condition. std::condition_variable_any may wake up
spuriously, and with libc++ on Windows the wait regularly returns a
little before the deadline as measured by steady_clock (coarse system
timer), in which case wait_for() reports std::cv_status::no_timeout.
In MSYS2 CLANG64 the assertion failed in roughly one of four runs:

  mutex_test.cc:32:Assertion
  - Expression: !_cond.wait_for (_mutex, std::chrono::milliseconds (1000))

Keep exercising the timed wait, but only check that the mutex is held
again afterwards. The signalled cases below are unchanged.
---
 libs/pbd/test/mutex_test.cc | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/libs/pbd/test/mutex_test.cc b/libs/pbd/test/mutex_test.cc
index 69be477..7ce50e8 100644
--- a/libs/pbd/test/mutex_test.cc
+++ b/libs/pbd/test/mutex_test.cc
@@ -29,7 +29,14 @@ MutexTest::test_cond ()
 {
 	PBD::Mutex::Lock lm (_mutex);
 
-	CPPUNIT_ASSERT (!_cond.wait_for (_mutex, std::chrono::milliseconds (1000)));
+	/* Nobody signals, so this normally times out. Do not assert on the
+	 * result though: condition variables may wake up spuriously, and
+	 * libc++ reports no_timeout whenever the wait returns before the
+	 * deadline by its steady clock, which happens now and then with the
+	 * coarse Windows timer resolution.
+	 */
+	(void) _cond.wait_for (_mutex, std::chrono::milliseconds (1000));
+	CPPUNIT_ASSERT (lm.locked ());
 
 	thread t1 ([&]() { _cond.signal (); });
 	_cond.wait (_mutex);
